WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:13.639
What do you think are the biggest, most important
threats to today's civilization?

00:00:13.639 --> 00:00:26.890
I think the biggest threat to our civilization
is very much tied, in my judgement, to the

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:36.150
infinity of human desire that hasn't found
its proper resting place.

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:45.180
And so, the restlessness that characterizes
us in search for transcendence is transposed

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:55.400
onto created reality, and we inevitably end
up in competitive relationships, investing

00:00:55.400 --> 00:01:01.760
the works of our hands and our own lives with
the kind of meaning that they cannot deliver.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:12.390
I think, unless we learn that human beings
cannot live by bread alone, we really cannot

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:22.579
sufficiently understand human beings, we really
cannot save, so to speak, human beings.

00:01:22.579 --> 00:01:32.369
We are meeting here at a time when, in Cleveland,
Ohio, there is the convention of the Republican

00:01:32.369 --> 00:01:40.159
Party, where people are yelling that the political
opponent of the Democratic Pary, Hillary Clinton,

00:01:40.159 --> 00:01:47.649
should be locked up.

00:01:47.649 --> 00:01:56.069
What is your take on the political situation
here in America, coming from Croatia and knowing

00:01:56.069 --> 00:01:59.810
a little bit about civil wars?

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:04.340
I'm quite fearful about the situation.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:11.560
I recently spoke in Munich at Together for
Europe, a gathering of a variety of Christian

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.360
communities.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:20.810
I opened my speech on the square in Munich
with a comment that the situation in Europe

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:28.940
today reminds me a bit of the situation just
before the civil war in former Yugoslavia,

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:38.750
where you had this sense of rage that had
amassed itself and was highly oppositional,

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:46.790
claiming its own identities and wanting to
move apart, assert itself over against others,

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:53.950
with very little interest in deliberation
about a common future.

00:02:53.950 --> 00:03:01.230
What one finds in many places in Europe today,
what one finds certainly at the Republican

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:09.620
convention right now is a red-hot anger that
is highly oppositional, that knows how to

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:16.840
destroy the opponent, and therefore also knows
how to build tall walls, but does not know

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:18.530
how to build bridges.

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:25.200
As it turns out, we live in highly pluralistic
societies, and without the ability to build

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:29.370
bridges we are unable to survive.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:37.180
I'm afraid that, in the situation where we
find ourselves today, this hightening of rhetoric,

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:46.681
of angry rhetoric, of destroying the enemy,
will in fact undermine our ability to live

00:03:46.681 --> 00:03:48.260
together as a nation.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:56.540
It is highly polarized; we need to heal the
nation rather than build further walls that separate us.

